{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And you shall consume all the peoples.</b> This is a positive commandment. And Scripture concludes by saying <b>which HaShem your Elohim gives you</b> — from this you learn that if you are lazy, you are spurning the gift of Elohim." "And the verse's saying <b>your eye shall not pity</b> etc. — this is in the manner of what they said (Proverbs 12:10): וְרַחֲמֵי רְשָׁעִים אַכְזָרִי — meaning, this is not among the good traits but an evil trait. Therefore He warns against [treating] a good trait as an adversary, for in the matter before us it is nothing but evil. Or perhaps He needed to warn ״לֹא תָחוֹס״ because HaShem blessed Israel with children, and they of blessed memory said (Sanhedrin 36b) that one who has children, his compassion increases, and the proof is that they appoint to the Sanhedrin only one who has children. Therefore the word of HaShem comes to counter the nature that is born in them through the power of the blessing of children." "And Scripture concludes by saying <b>and you shall not serve</b> etc., to alert you that compassion in this matter will give birth, from the sense of it, to idolatry. This is what it means by <b>for it is a snare to you</b> — meaning the people: for when you pity them, you act cruelly toward yourself. Or its meaning turns back to the compassion that He commanded him not to pity."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:15:50.322970802Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}